# Auto-build on task success

When you have completed all the editing and testing for a task and are about to tell the user "done", run [`build_exe.bat`](../../build_exe.bat) via the Shell tool BEFORE handing off. The user wants to immediately double-click the freshly built EXE to verify the change in the real, packaged binary they ship - not just in a dev-mode `python app.py` run.

## Required pre-conditions

Trigger the build only when ALL of the following are true:

- Every code or asset change requested by the user has landed.
- The relevant test suite (`pytest`) is green; lint/type errors introduced by the change are fixed.
- You are about to send the final summary message of the turn.

## Skip cases

You MAY skip the auto-build only when one of these is unambiguously true:

- The change is documentation-only (Markdown / `.cursor/rules/` / docstrings) and does not touch any file that ends up inside the EXE.
- The user explicitly said "don't build the EXE" / "skip the build" / "neskládej exe" / equivalent.
- You're running in an environment that cannot execute Windows batch files (non-Windows agent host); leave a one-line note in the final reply explaining the skip.

## Procedure

1. Spawn the build with the Shell tool: `build_exe.bat` from the repo root. Use a long `block_until_ms` (60 000 ms) because the first build of the day rebuilds the venv and downloads Playwright Chromium.
2. Wait for the script to finish. If it prints `BUILD OK`, mention `dist\ApplyPilotAI.exe` in your final message so the user can launch it directly.
3. If it prints `BUILD FAILED`, treat it as a regression in the work you just did. Read the tail of the build output, fix the root cause (typical culprits: missing `--hidden-import`, broken import in `app.py`, new data folder not in `--add-data`), re-run the build, and only then tell the user "done". Do NOT report success on a failed build.

## Forbidden

- Reporting the task as complete while `build_exe.bat` is still failing or has never been run for the change.
- Editing `build_exe.bat` to comment out the failing PyInstaller flag instead of fixing the underlying issue.
- Skipping the build because it "takes too long" - use the long `block_until_ms` and wait it out.
